Why This Is Asked
Assess the ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued semantics in a constrained CSP and to design a propagation/backtracking approach that handles unknowns.

Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬T=F, ¬F=T, ¬U=U; ∧/∨ use min/max under T>U>F; A→B := ¬A ∨ B.
- Constraint propagation in a small CSP with unknowns; existence of a model containing U.
- Backtracking strategies to prune non-viable branches.

Code Example
```javascript
const V = {T:'T', F:'F', U:'U'}
functionNot(x){ if (x==='T') return 'F'; if (x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ if (a==='T' || b==='T') return 'T'; if (a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U'; return 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ if (a==='F' || b==='F') return 'F'; if (a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U'; return 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ return kleeneOr(functionNot(a), b) }
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 3-valued dataflow reasoning, fixpoint propagation, and translating logic into a workable algorithm.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic (T, F, U)
- Worklist/fixpoint propagation
- Dataflow dependency graphs
- Handling cycles and convergence

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued logic
const T = true, F = false, U = 'U';
function not(x){ if (x===T) return F; if (x===F) return T; return U; }
function and(a,b){ if (a===F || b===F) return F; if (a===T && b===T) return T; return U; }
function or(a,b){ if (a===T || b===T) return T; if (a===F && b===F) return F; return U; }

// program: A = B AND C; D = NOT E; F = D OR A
let B = U, C = T, E = U;
let A = and(B, C);
let D = not(E);
let F = or(D, A);
```

